From: eirikr <151315375+Oichkatzelesfrettschen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:20:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] pl022: settle the frames on the wire at register
 accesses

A one-shot clock-source timer ended each frame handed to a registered
device, and a driver waiting for room in a full transmit FIFO read SSPSR in
a loop until the timer fired: 12.7 status reads per byte at the SD card's
bit rate, 14.7 register accesses per byte in all, each one leaving
translated code for the bus. The first frame written to an idle controller
also ended early: a 640 ns frame stayed busy for 0 to 10 status polls of
about 24 ns.

The end time of each frame on the wire now sits in a queue, and a read of
SSPSR or a write of SSPDR retires the frames that have ended by the time of
that access. The time is the accessing core's own clock: its time handle
plus the instructions executed since the handle was last read exactly
(both leave out the translation block in flight, so the time is that of
the start of the block making the access),
because the machine's time source advances only when every core has
reported and so stands still inside a time grant. An access from the
monitor reads the machine's time. A status read that finds the transmit
FIFO full skips the reading core to the end of the frame in the shift
register and returns with TNF set, so the wait takes the same virtual time
as the polling loop with 4.0 accesses per byte; such a read lasts up to
one frame time and delays an interrupt by as much. Every frame lasts its
bit time, the first of a burst included (24 polls). FIFO levels, BSY and
the discard of a write to a full FIFO are unchanged.
